WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:02.550
Friendship, family, and fun…

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.590
What do these words have in common?

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.060
They’re integral parts of the human experience,

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.410
and also share the same first letter.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.800
Our ancestors thousands of years in the past

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.099
may have struggled to pronounce these words,

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.140
as the “f” sound would have been cumbersome
to produce.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.920
Sounds like “f” or “v” are called

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.560
labiodentals: labio, relating to your lips,

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:27.369
and dentals, your teeth.

00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:29.360
They are produced when you raise your bottom

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.620
lip to touch your upper teeth.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.940
Labiodentals are used in over half of the

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.260
languages spoken around the world today, but

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.920
before shifts in food-processing technology,

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.780
these sounds may not have been as common.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.080
Before the Neolithic, food was minimally processed,

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.680
and required more effort to chew.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.340
This constant gnashing meant teeth wore down

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.320
more, resulting in what is known as an edge-to-edge

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.120
bite by adolescence.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.719
Then came the emergence of food processing
technologies.

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:02.320
Gone were the days of laborious chewing, as

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.440
stews, preserved food, and ground grain as flour

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.420
became more common.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:09.640
Softer food meant less wear and tear on the

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.100
teeth, and the preservation of an overbite,

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.100
a vertical overlap of the teeth and an overjet,

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.960
horizontal overlapping of the teeth, which

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.160
humans are normally born with.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.660
Researchers created a biomechanical model

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.500
of an edge-to-edge bite, aligned top and bottom

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.341
teeth, and an overjet and overbite to see

00:01:30.341 --> 00:01:34.560
if one provided an advantage for articulating labiodentals.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.660
They found that the edge-to-edge configuration

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.590
would have required more effort to produce

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.200
a labiodental, as the distance from the bottom

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.780
lip and top teeth would have been greater.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.600
The preservation of an overbite and overjet

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.940
also increased the likelihood that a labiodental

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.540
would be made on accident--like saying “f”

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.650
instead of “p.”

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.120
Today, nearly all societies have access to

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.520
softer foods, and most people retain their

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.620
overbite and overjet throughout their lifetimes.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.860
This makes producing labiodentals easier,

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.610
but historically not all languages have had

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:11.110
these sounds.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:14.460
Nowadays, many languages are acquiring labiodental

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.560
sounds like “f” and “v” through contact

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.820
with other languages that have them.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.400
One example includes West Greenlandic speakers

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.260
who have been in long sustained contact with

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.700
European languages, including Danish.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.600
This study shows how language can be shaped

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.440
by changes in human biology.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.640
In the future, researchers hope to better

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.220
understand not only how this language change

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:41.480
took place, but why.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.700
Some data suggests that labiodentals served

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.030
as an indicator of social class, proving advantageous

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:49.500
to those who adopted the sound.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:56.000
In the meantime, thank your local food processor
for being able to say those “f words”

